|~ `::excerpt`, the kind of a value told within a room of characters,
   and what is declared under it: the keys the tag of the value told
   writes, and its laws.
   The rules it holds: [QSPRN#excerpt], [QSERR#alert]. ~|

:excerpt
  |~~ An excerpt tells a value too large for its room in the words of the
      tag the value stands under, which gives its own and counts it itself:
      a vector and a set `:count`, `:first` and `:last`, the elements at its
      two ends that fit whole, `[0 1 2 … 3409]` told as
      `::excerpt{:count 3410 :first [0 1 2 3] :last [3408 3409]}`, a map its
      entries at its two ends the same way, and either, where an end holds
      no part whole, `:head` and `:tail`, the ends of its print; a string
      `:characters`, `:head` and `:tail`, its first and its last characters;
      a trace `:failed`, the step that failed at each level, `:paths` and
      `:subject`, the value the step that failed met; and any other value the
      first and the last characters of its print. A count stands where parts
      are left out, and the excerpt a tag gives holds no excerpt. A tag of the
      reader gives its own by `excerpt` under its label. `excerpt` answers
      one, and a host prints a fact of a failure past the room of a fact so,
      the failure then as the excerpt of its facts under the tag of its
      refusal, `::X::excerpt{…}`, and an answer past the budget of every
      answer within its refusal.

      An excerpt is a map under its tag, read by its keys, `get /count`, and
      a verb meets it as the map it is, never as the value it tells. The
      whole is read live, a fact of a failure by `!|` and an answer past the
      budget by `answer` of the cell that keeps it.
      ::link~(::any/excerpt | doc) ::link~(::qlang/hosts | doc)
  ~~|
  ::kind
    | accepts |~~ The keys the tag of the value told writes. ~~| ::map
    | throws :PayloadKindNotTakenError
        |~~ An excerpt is the map of the keys the tag of the value it tells
            writes, which `excerpt` answers, `x | excerpt`.
        ~~|
    | proves :tellsAVectorByItsEnds
        |~~ A vector tells its count and the elements at its two ends. ~~|
        ~(3410 | range | excerpt | get [/count ~(get /first | first) ~(get /last | last)] | eq [3410 0 3409])
    | proves :readsBackAsItself
        |~~ An excerpt prints and reads back as itself. ~~|
        ~(3410 | range | excerpt | :e it | print | parse | open | eq e)
